{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>To Aaron, and to his sons, and to all the children of Israel</b> — Since the passage is an admonition to offer all sacrifices at the Tent of Meeting, He admonishes the priests, who are the ones who offer the sacrifice, not to offer them outside, and He admonishes Israel as well, that they should bring them to HaShem there and not offer them on their own outside. And the correct understanding of the passage is what our Sages mentioned (Hullin 16a), that He forbade meat of desire to Israel in the wilderness, and they may eat only peace offerings that are made upon the altar of HaShem. Therefore He said that anyone who slaughters from the three species of animals from which all sacrifices are brought — which are ox, sheep, and goat — they must bring them all to the entrance of the Tent of Meeting and make them sacrifices of peace offerings, to offer the fat and the blood upon the altar of HaShem, and then he may eat the meat according to the law. And He said that if he slaughters them in another place he will be liable to excision (כרת). And this is close to the language of the verse, for at first He imposed excision upon all who slaughter outside, even unconsecrated meat (חולין), and this is the prohibition against meat of desire, and He gave the reason: in order that they dedicate them to HaShem, and the priest dashes the blood upon the altar of HaShem and offers up the fat upon it, and afterwards (verses 8–9) He admonished that he not make an altar to HaShem outside and offer upon it the peace offerings mentioned or a burnt offering, like the matter done with consecrated animals at the time when the bamot (high places) were permitted — but only at the entrance of the Tent of Meeting he shall offer them to HaShem. So He restricted all slaughterings, both unconsecrated and consecrated, to be all within. Therefore He said in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 12:10–11), 'When you cross the Jordan and settle in the land… then there shall be the place that HaShem will choose; there you shall bring your burnt offerings and your sacrifices,' and He said (ibid. verses 13–14), 'Take heed to yourself lest you offer your burnt offerings in every place that you see, but only in the place,' and so on, so that the prohibition of slaughter outside for consecrated animals remains in its place at the time of the Chosen House, but He permitted it for unconsecrated meat. This is what He said (ibid. verse 15), 'Only with all your soul's desire you may slaughter and eat meat,' and so on — to say that in the land you shall not follow the restraint mentioned here, that one may not slaughter unconsecrated meat at all and that everything must be made peace offerings upon the altar of HaShem. And He explained there the reason (Deuteronomy 12:20), 'When HaShem your Elohim enlarges your border,' and so on — to say that the prohibition was at first when they were in the wilderness, since it would be easy for them to bring all their sacrifices to the entrance of the Tent of Meeting, but after He enlarges their border they may eat meat of desire and slaughter in their towns, but not consecrated animals. And this is the reason for what is mentioned here, 'who slaughters in the camp or who slaughters outside the camp' — He mentions also those who slaughter outside the camp, that they are obligated to bring them to the entrance of the Tent of Meeting, for even though HaShem will permit meat of desire when He enlarges their border because they are not near the place of the Sanctuary, He will not permit such in the wilderness even outside the camp, because they are not as far from the altar as they will be in the land that HaShem will enlarge for their border, for then He will permit them meat of desire. And this is the opinion of Rabbi Yishmael (Hullin 16a) in these passages, and from here he said: from the very beginning meat of desire was forbidden to them, and this is what fits the verse according to the plain sense (פשט). And this is their statement in the Aggadah (Bereshit Rabbah 4:6): the Rabbis say — many things did the Holy One, blessed be He, forbid them, and then went back and permitted them in another place. Know this for yourself: the Holy One, blessed be He, forbade Israel to slaughter and eat until he brings it to the entrance of the Tent of Meeting — from where? For it is written (Leviticus 17:4), 'and to the entrance of the Tent of Meeting,' and so on. What is written there? 'Blood shall be imputed to that man,' and so on. And here He went back and permitted it to them, for it is written (Deuteronomy 12:20), 'With all your soul's desire you may eat meat' — from where? From what they read in that context, 'When HaShem your Elohim enlarges.' And what Rashi wrote (Rashi on Leviticus 17:3), 'The verse speaks of consecrated animals,' and so on — that is the midrash of the one who says (Hullin 16a) that meat of desire was not forbidden at all, and according to his opinion the baraita was taught in the Torah of the Priests (i.e., Sifra, here). But the way of settling the verses according to their modes and the way of the Sages in the Aggadot and in most places is as we have explained."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "The abbreviation רבותינו and rabbinic references are rendered as 'our Sages' following standard convention. Bereshit Rabbah is cited as דב\"ר, resolved to its commonly used form. The Aramaic phrases from the Gemara and Midrash are translated into English rather than left in the original."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:54:58.551370204Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}